The most fundamental hash map pattern is **store and lookup**: as you iterate through the array, store what you've seen in a map. For each new element, ask the map: 'is there something already stored that, combined with this element, gives me my answer?'

## Two Sum - The Canonical Example

For each element `arr[i]`, ask: 'have I already seen `target - arr[i]`?' If yes, return the stored index and `i`. If no, store `arr[i] → i`.

```javascript
function twoSum(arr, target) {
  const seen = new Map(); // value → index
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const complement = target - arr[i];
    if (seen.has(complement)) return [seen.get(complement), i];
    seen.set(arr[i], i);
  }
  return [];
}
```

This pattern generalizes broadly. 'Find two elements with minimum difference' → store elements in a sorted structure. 'Find a pair with product k' → store elements and look up `k / arr[i]`. The shape is always: iterate, look up complement, store current.

One of the most powerful combinations in array problems: **prefix sum stored in a hash map** enables 'find subarray with sum equal to k' in O(n).

## The Key Insight

`prefixSum[j] - prefixSum[i] = k` means the subarray `arr[i+1..j]` has sum `k`. Rearranging: `prefixSum[i] = prefixSum[j] - k`. So for each position `j`, we want to know: 'how many times has `currentPrefix - k` appeared as a prefix sum before?'

```javascript
function subarraySumEqualsK(arr, k) {
  const prefixCount = new Map([[0, 1]]); // prefix 0 seen once (empty prefix)
  let count = 0, prefix = 0;
  for (const x of arr) {
    prefix += x;
    count += prefixCount.get(prefix - k) || 0; // how many valid starting points
    prefixCount.set(prefix, (prefixCount.get(prefix) || 0) + 1);
  }
  return count;
}
```

The `Map([[0, 1]])` initialization handles subarrays starting at index 0. This pattern extends to 'number of subarrays with sum divisible by k' (store `prefix % k`) and 'longest subarray with equal 0s and 1s' (convert 0→-1, store first occurrence of each prefix sum).

